id: pm-gh1604
title: "GH-1604: Classify missing linked-test executables separately from assertion failures"
description: "**Version:** pm 2026.10.26 (Node 24.19.0), plain non-git tracker.\n\n**Repro**\n```sh\npm init\npm create --type Task --title t            # -> pm-xxxx\npm test pm-xxxx --add 'command=definitely-not-a-binary-xyz'\npm test pm-xxxx --run --json\n```\n**Observed:** `{\"exit_code\":127,\"failure_category\":\"assertion_failure\",\"status\":\"failed\"}` with stderr `/bin/sh: 1: definitely-not-a-binary-xyz: not found`. An ordinary `node -e \"process.exit(1)\"` gets the same category, and `npm test` without a package manifest (stderr reports `ENOENT`) is also `assertion_failure`.\n\n**Expected:** a test whose executable cannot be resolved (shell exit 127 with the command named as not found), or whose runner cannot start, is classified separately from an executed test that failed — e.g. a documented `command_not_found` / setup category, or the existing `spawn_error` extended to shell resolution failures. Keep exit code and stderr unchanged.\n\n**Why it matters for agents:** agents route on `failure_category`. Today a missing tool or broken test command is routed to \"fix the implementation\", and the agent needs a second read of stderr (≈2.7 KB here) to discover it is a setup problem. Classifying narrowly (exit 127 + the named executable is missing) avoids misreading arbitrary child stderr that contains \"not found\".\n\n\nIntake boundary: this is a report against published 2026.10.26, not proof of a current-checkout defect. Reproduce in an external sandbox before implementation. Related completed work retains its shipped scope. Proposed examples or categories are not accepted contracts until validated.\n\nSource: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1604"
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[3]: agent-ux,github,sdk
